  "body": "What happens: on a Windows checkout, `catalog_2d_drill_overfeed::regenerates` and `catalog_2d_drill_overfeed::rebuilds` fail. The generated refusal line ends with LF. The golden file `examples/catalog/2d/drill-overfeed/refusal.txt` ends with CRLF on disk. The assert reads: \"2d/drill-overfeed: refusal lines differ from refusal.txt\".\n\nEvidence: `git ls-files --eol` shows `i/lf w/crlf attr/` for that file. The blob in git is LF; the checkout wrote CRLF. `.gitattributes` pins `examples/**/*.nc` to LF (the \"pinned byte for byte\" rule) but no rule covers `examples/catalog/**/refusal.txt`. The refusal test compares the lines byte for byte (commit 37332ffb0, \"feat(catalog): a catalog case can expect a refusal\").\n\nWhere it came from: the quick gate of the review fixes on PR #3499 (log `gate-logs/quick-20261123-045105`, step \"app suites: example_catalog\"). The diff of that PR touches only multiaxis code. The failure needs no code change: the committed LF bytes restored on disk make the suite pass (37 passed, 0 failed).\n\nPrior case of the same class: #3119 (closed, shipped in v0.25.0). That fix made the read tolerant of CRLF. This one guards a byte-exact golden, so the rule side is the likely fix: pin the catalog refusal goldens to LF in every checkout.\n\nDone means: a fresh Windows checkout passes `cargo test -p camcraft-app --test example_catalog` with no manual fix of the file bytes.\n\nProposed labels (I cannot set labels): P5 (a gate fix that blocks Windows contributors), effort:low, area:build, source:agent.\n",
